00:05:31.655 --> 00:05:33.780
MANOJ GUNTI: So this is
very interesting, isn't it?

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:39.760
So in the whole video, you
have not seen anywhere mentions

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:43.910
about access, about
integration, and all the stuff.

00:05:43.910 --> 00:05:48.040
So what we are talking today
is about creating experiences

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:51.820
for customers, creating
digital transactions,

00:05:51.820 --> 00:05:56.650
and actually enabling digital
transformation through APIs.

00:05:56.650 --> 00:05:59.800
And we are not seeing
this trend just

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:01.990
with AccuWeather or some
of the other customers.

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:04.930
But in everyday,
day-to-day conversations

00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:07.210
that we are having
with our customers,

00:06:07.210 --> 00:06:10.510
we are seeing that
the topics of API

00:06:10.510 --> 00:06:15.640
management have evolved beyond
just access and integration.

00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:19.450
Like enterprises today are
exposing their core business

00:06:19.450 --> 00:06:19.990
assets.

00:06:19.990 --> 00:06:22.000
And if you take the
example of AccuWeather,

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:25.690
their core business asset
is the weather data.

00:06:25.690 --> 00:06:30.010
And they are exposing them
to a fleet of developers,

00:06:30.010 --> 00:06:34.360
and also several businesses that
are actually using this weather

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:37.030
data through APIs,
and building them

00:06:37.030 --> 00:06:39.430
into their business
applications, as well

00:06:39.430 --> 00:06:41.620
as consumer applications.

00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:45.460
And AccuWeather it is actually
monetizing those APIs.

00:06:45.460 --> 00:06:50.650
So it has essentially triggered
a new business channel

00:06:50.650 --> 00:06:54.640
for the enterprise, leading
to unlocking newer markets, as

00:06:54.640 --> 00:06:58.040
well as additional revenue
streams for the organization.

00:06:58.040 --> 00:07:02.660
So that is how APIs have
evolved over the last few years.

00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:06.490
And when you think of
APIs actually transforming

00:07:06.490 --> 00:07:09.610
businesses, what we also
see is several customers

00:07:09.610 --> 00:07:13.090
are-- several prospective
customers are actually

00:07:13.090 --> 00:07:16.510
still thinking of APIs
as just a gateway.

00:07:16.510 --> 00:07:19.480
But when you are doing
business critical stuff,

00:07:19.480 --> 00:07:21.790
I think it is very,
very important

00:07:21.790 --> 00:07:26.890
to start thinking of APIs as
not just one-off projects,

00:07:26.890 --> 00:07:31.300
but to actually think of them
as business applications, right?

00:07:31.300 --> 00:07:35.290
Because you're not
just providing access

00:07:35.290 --> 00:07:37.780
within your business
systems internally,

00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:40.540
but you are now actually
reaching out to your customers

00:07:40.540 --> 00:07:41.560
through APIs.

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:45.130
And these products, when you
are treating them as a business

00:07:45.130 --> 00:07:49.000
application, need to
have a full lifecycle

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:53.560
management to be incorporated,
but not just a simple gateway.

00:07:53.560 --> 00:07:56.920
Because the fact that a simple
gateway cannot give you all

00:07:56.920 --> 00:07:58.180
the security levels.

00:07:58.180 --> 00:08:01.030
It cannot give you all the
monitoring capabilities.

00:08:01.030 --> 00:08:02.950
And most importantly,
it will not

00:08:02.950 --> 00:08:05.200
give you the
capability to make it

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:08.270
easily consumable for a
wide range of developers.

00:08:08.270 --> 00:08:11.920
So what does a full lifecycle
API management platform entail?

00:08:11.920 --> 00:08:15.100
So it starts all the
way from the ability

00:08:15.100 --> 00:08:20.830
to create a proxy, design, test,
all the way until publishing.

00:08:20.830 --> 00:08:23.530
And making sure
that it is secure,

00:08:23.530 --> 00:08:26.320
making sure that the right
people have the right access

00:08:26.320 --> 00:08:28.000
to the right APIs.

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:30.550
And also, at the
same time, to do

00:08:30.550 --> 00:08:32.620
a lot of analytics
in the backend

00:08:32.620 --> 00:08:36.909
to analyze, potentially, how are
developers consuming the APIs?

00:08:36.909 --> 00:08:40.000
And are the APIs
available, up and running?

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:43.450
And how do I protect them
against abuse from [? bot ?]

00:08:43.450 --> 00:08:46.040
and the internal and
external [INAUDIBLE]??

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:51.040
So what the evolution of APIs
and treating APIs as a product

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:56.380
means is that now it is not
just an API developer who is

00:08:56.380 --> 00:08:58.050
involved in the whole program.

00:08:58.050 --> 00:09:00.640
So a wide range of
stakeholders now

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:03.190
get involved into this
whole program, all the way

00:09:03.190 --> 00:09:07.980
from a product or a program
manager to a security or IT Ops

00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:11.090
person, to the line of business.

00:09:11.090 --> 00:09:16.750
If I were the product or program
manager of a large API program,

00:09:16.750 --> 00:09:20.650
then I am very much interested
in understanding which

00:09:20.650 --> 00:09:23.260
APIs are my developers using?

00:09:23.260 --> 00:09:26.650
What are the most
popular API [INAUDIBLE]??

00:09:26.650 --> 00:09:30.250
How do I increase the
overall developer engagement?

00:09:30.250 --> 00:09:32.870
And for sure, I
will have to report

00:09:32.870 --> 00:09:36.190
the success of my program
to my senior management.

00:09:36.190 --> 00:09:38.990
And how do I very quickly
and easily do that?

00:09:38.990 --> 00:09:43.180
And from an IT operations
and security perspective,

00:09:43.180 --> 00:09:45.970
what happens is if your
mission critical or business

00:09:45.970 --> 00:09:48.970
critical applications
are powered through API.

00:09:48.970 --> 00:09:52.180
Let's take an example
of a recent scenario

00:09:52.180 --> 00:09:55.750
where your promotions,
or your payments,

00:09:55.750 --> 00:09:58.960
or your product catalog
availability [? reveals ?]

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:01.780
everything is powered
by a different API.

00:10:01.780 --> 00:10:04.960
And imagine you are in a
Black Friday and Cyber Monday

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:05.980
situation.

00:10:05.980 --> 00:10:10.390
And even a slight
performance delay of an API

00:10:10.390 --> 00:10:13.990
could lead to significant
loss in your revenue.

00:10:13.990 --> 00:10:16.780
How do I, as an
operations person,

00:10:16.780 --> 00:10:20.560
make sure that my APIs are
always available, they are

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:22.840
always performing as expected?

00:10:22.840 --> 00:10:25.930
And at the same time,
if something goes wrong,

00:10:25.930 --> 00:10:29.440
how do I quickly diagnose
the root cause of this API

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:33.350
and bring it back to
life as soon as possible?

00:10:33.350 --> 00:10:36.770
And from a security and
[INAUDIBLE] perspective,

00:10:36.770 --> 00:10:39.950
how do I make sure that
all the developers that

00:10:39.950 --> 00:10:42.800
are building these
amazing APIs adhere

00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:45.290
to the security policies
of my organization,

00:10:45.290 --> 00:10:47.060
as well as my industry?

00:10:47.060 --> 00:10:49.160
And of course, you will
not be able to scale up

00:10:49.160 --> 00:10:53.720
any of your API programs without
the buy-in and investment

00:10:53.720 --> 00:10:55.260
from the line of business.

00:10:55.260 --> 00:11:02.210
And if I were the VP of IT,
the CIO, CTO, or even the CFO,

00:11:02.210 --> 00:11:04.430
some of my biggest
concerns would

00:11:04.430 --> 00:11:07.220
be, what is the ROI
of the API program

00:11:07.220 --> 00:11:08.480
that I have invested in?

00:11:08.480 --> 00:11:09.980
How do I measure it?

00:11:09.980 --> 00:11:13.910
Where shall I invest more, and
which API should I actually--

00:11:13.910 --> 00:11:16.650
which API programs
should I actually retire?

00:11:16.650 --> 00:11:20.450
And what is the business
impact or the consumer impact

00:11:20.450 --> 00:11:22.970
of this whole investment
that I'm making?

00:11:22.970 --> 00:11:25.610
Am I able to reach
out to newer markets?

00:11:25.610 --> 00:11:27.815
Can I unlock new
revenue streams?

00:11:27.815 --> 00:11:31.030
Or what else can I do with this?

00:11:31.030 --> 00:11:34.590
So what is happening
on the other side

00:11:34.590 --> 00:11:40.470
is as you build and
scale your API programs,

00:11:40.470 --> 00:11:44.190
you actually start to
touch each and every person

00:11:44.190 --> 00:11:46.110
in the API lifecycle.

00:11:46.110 --> 00:11:48.210
What we call an API
lifecycle is all

00:11:48.210 --> 00:11:51.240
the way from the backend
system that you're exposing,

00:11:51.240 --> 00:11:53.850
to the API teams that
are building the APIs,

00:11:53.850 --> 00:11:56.550
and the developer that is
using those APIs to build

00:11:56.550 --> 00:12:00.720
amazing apps, and the consumer
who is actually using the app,

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:03.930
and indirectly using the
APIs that are behind it.

00:12:03.930 --> 00:12:07.140
So across all these touchpoints,
as you build and scale

00:12:07.140 --> 00:12:10.320
the programs, there's
a lot of data that has

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:13.230
been collected on the platform.

00:12:13.230 --> 00:12:16.050
And this is one of the
other big advantages

00:12:16.050 --> 00:12:19.110
that you get when compared
to an API gateway.

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:21.510
An API management
platform is essentially

00:12:21.510 --> 00:12:25.320
able to collect all this
data and able to provide you

00:12:25.320 --> 00:12:27.360
with the right insights
that will address

00:12:27.360 --> 00:12:31.060
some of the problems that we
discussed in the earlier slide.

00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:33.360
So Apigee API
Management Platform

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:35.850
is a full lifecycle
API management platform

00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:39.480
that has several layers,
including the runtime layer,

00:12:39.480 --> 00:12:44.310
the mediation engine, the
API analytics space, and also

00:12:44.310 --> 00:12:46.120
the developer ecosystem.

00:12:46.120 --> 00:12:50.030
So if we look at the
API analytics space,

00:12:50.030 --> 00:12:55.350
Apigee today provides several
out of the box inbuilt

00:12:55.350 --> 00:12:58.157
reports that help all
the different personas

00:12:58.157 --> 00:12:59.032
that we talked about.

00:12:59.032 --> 00:13:01.980
It provides you developer
engagement metrics

00:13:01.980 --> 00:13:07.290
and the API program metrics
for the API product manager

00:13:07.290 --> 00:13:09.150
or program manager persona.

00:13:09.150 --> 00:13:11.010
And it provides
business metrics,

00:13:11.010 --> 00:13:14.280
such as the revenue report,
the customer churn, et cetera,

00:13:14.280 --> 00:13:16.110
for the line of business.

00:13:16.110 --> 00:13:18.870
And it provides you
operational metrics,

00:13:18.870 --> 00:13:22.290
like the availability, the
performance, the ability

00:13:22.290 --> 00:13:25.980
to quickly diagnose
issues, linking your alerts

00:13:25.980 --> 00:13:28.020
to the right
playbook so that you

00:13:28.020 --> 00:13:30.700
can take the appropriate
remedial actions.

00:13:30.700 --> 00:13:34.980
And also, security reporting,
which is about adherence

00:13:34.980 --> 00:13:41.250
to compliance policies, and also
analyzing the runtime traffic.

00:13:41.250 --> 00:13:45.180
And at the same time, looking
at the activity of the users

00:13:45.180 --> 00:13:47.400
within your organization,
whether they're accessing

